<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
    <dict>
        <key>Description</key>
        <string>NOT WORKING (expected). This recipe is expected to stop at its first run and publish nothing; whether it stays in the repository is the owner's decision. The download answers 404: on 2026-10-04 the file at the address that this recipe builds, slack-standalone-&lt;version&gt;.0.msi in the folder of the version, existed for the 13 versions of Slack's release notes from 4.46.96 (2025-09-09) to 4.48.102 (2026-03-17) and answered 404 for the 13 versions from 4.49.81 (2026-04-02) to 4.52.171 (2026-09-28) (the 26 newest of the page; older ones were not tried), and Slack's own address for the MSI (the api.desktop.latestRelease call with variant msi) names the same missing file; no other address of an MSI could be established, so the pair publishes nothing until Slack serves the MSI again (the recipe then works unchanged). This download is NOT verified yet: Slack publishes no hash for the file, and the shared signature check (AuthenticodeVerifier) is not part of this recipe, so nothing proves that the file is what Slack published. The shared importer therefore refuses a real run of the cimian recipe unless the override sets cimian_allow_unverified to true (pcman_allow_unverified for the package tool's recipe). First reads the newest version from Slack's release notes page for Windows (the first match of the pattern on the page), then downloads the per-machine Slack installer for Windows (the 64-bit MSI) from the address that has this version in it; the download is refused when a redirect leads to anything but https (curl_opts --proto-redir =https), because with no hash to check the transfer has only TLS to protect it. The first run with AutoPkg 2.9.0, on 2026-10-04, passed the first step (the page and the pattern: the first match on the page is the newest version, 4.52.171 that day) and stopped at the second, with a 404. The recipe never stops on an unchanged download, so a failed import is tried again at the next run.</string>
        <key>Identifier</key>
        <string>com.github.serrc-techops.download.Slack-Win</string>
        <key>Input</key>
        <dict>
            <key>NAME</key>
            <string>Slack</string>
            <key>RELEASE_NOTES_URL</key>
            <string>https://slack.com/release-notes/windows</string>
            <key>VERSION_PATTERN</key>
            <string>Slack\s+(?P&lt;version&gt;[0-9]+\.[0-9]+\.[0-9]+)</string>
        </dict>
        <key>MinimumVersion</key>
        <string>1.0.0</string>
        <key>Process</key>
        <array>
            <dict>
                <key>Processor</key>
                <string>URLTextSearcher</string>
                <key>Arguments</key>
                <dict>
                    <key>url</key>
                    <string>%RELEASE_NOTES_URL%</string>
                    <key>re_pattern</key>
                    <string>%VERSION_PATTERN%</string>
                </dict>
            </dict>
            <dict>
                <key>Processor</key>
                <string>URLDownloader</string>
                <key>Arguments</key>
                <dict>
                    <key>url</key>
                    <string>https://downloads.slack-edge.com/desktop-releases/windows/x64/%version%/slack-standalone-%version%.0.msi</string>
                    <key>curl_opts</key>
                    <array>
                        <string>--proto-redir</string>
                        <string>=https</string>
                    </array>
                </dict>
            </dict>
            <dict>
                <key>Processor</key>
                <string>EndOfCheckPhase</string>
            </dict>
        </array>
    </dict>
</plist>
